The Bohr-union audit shows the proposed growth: the minimum of \(W_q/V_q^{3/2}\) rises from about \(-0.126\) at ambient size 4,096 to \(0.946\) at 200,000. This is evidence, not an infinite-family proof.

A positive Fejér-mixture variant looks more tractable for a proof: its nonnegative components give a lower bound for the cubic moment without estimating mixed triples. I’m checking the constants and an explicit selection rule while the benchmark and higher-moment audits run.

